@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using SpaceSails.Client.Components
@using SpaceSails.Core
@*
    #243 · THE CONDITIONS STRIP — the Nav screen's one meet-the-conditions readout.

    Unlike its neighbours in this directory this surface was NOT cut out of NavHud.razor; it is new, and it
    is fenced and shaped exactly like the ones that were, because the composition (#251) splices every file
    in this directory back into the HUD before a source guard reads it. A new surface that skipped the fence
    would be a block fifty-eight of Map's guards could no longer see.

    IHandleEvent repeats NavHud's own override, which repeats Map.Sim.cs's: the HUD's refresh belongs to
    OnTick's 200 ms throttle, and a child that did not repeat it would put an automatic render back on every
    event in here. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules; it exists to give the component
    Map.razor.css's scope (pinned in SpaceSails.Client.csproj), so every rule that styles these elements
    goes on matching them.

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block sits inside NavHud's Razor CODE block, and a
    code block is where its first statements are legal.
*@
@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
            @* #243 · THE CONDITIONS STRIP. Owner, on approach to the roadster: "Let's have a more clear
               display of how well we meet the criteria. Are we closing or distancing. … it is slow to read
               visually: read the scope, remember the limit was 3 from another part of the screen, then check
               the other variable. In the boarding dialog just seeing green or red told the story much
               faster." And the generalization, same session: "I guess it could be a general
               meet-the-conditions [display] in the navigation screen."

               ONE INSTRUMENT, NOT FOUR. The owner's own distillation is the design: "trying to be dockable,
               or capture etc — we always have the duo of relative speed and distance, and some criteria for
               those." So this strip is the duo, with whichever gate is live supplying the two limits —
               boarding window, clamp envelope, orbit capture, shuttle reach. Which one speaks, and every
               number in it, is decided in Core by ConditionsGate off the code that ENFORCES each gate; the
               markup here quotes no threshold and makes no comparison, which is what stops the readout from
               being green about a limit the machinery refuses.

               IT IS ABSENT, NOT EMPTY, WHEN NO GATE IS LIVE. The complaint being answered is a screen that
               has to be hunted across; a permanently reserved rectangle that is blank most of the time is one
               more thing to hunt past. ActiveConditions() returns null and this draws nothing at all.

               ITS HOUSING IS THE PIRACY HOUSING, for the same reason #200's clamp panel took it (#1043): the
               plunder offer, the boarding progress and the docking focus are all in-FLOW sections of this
               same .map-readouts block, not floating cards. A box that claims real document height in a
               column already bound to the window (.map-flowcolumn, #992) cannot fight the lines above it or
               the Plotting panel below it for a pixel — no z-index, no position, nothing to lose a stacking
               argument with, and #236's no-button zone is not even in play because the strip has no buttons.

               IT SITS ABOVE THE CLAMP PANEL, not below it: during an approach the strip is the summary and
               #200's panel is the detail, and the summary is read first. When the live gate IS the clamp the
               two say the same thing in the same words, because the strip's chips ARE DockFocus's rows. *@
            @if (ActiveConditions() is { } conditions)
            {
                <div class="map-conditions border border-secondary rounded px-2 py-1 mt-1">
                    <div class="fw-bold">@ConditionsGate.Title(conditions)</div>
                    @foreach (GateCriterion criterion in conditions.Criteria)
                    {
                        <CriteriaChip Criterion="@criterion" />
                    }
                </div>
            }
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}
